---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/ias/ias2.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        {
          id: "s1",
          label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage",
        },
        {
          id: "s2",
          label: "Scope & Applicability",
        },
        {
          id: "s3",
          label: "Recognition Criteria",
        },
        {
          id: "s4",
          label: "Cost of Inventories",
        },
        {
          id: "s5",
          label: "Cost Formulas",
        },
        {
          id: "s6",
          label: "Net Realisable Value",
        },
        {
          id: "s7",
          label: "Recognition as an Expense",
        },
        {
          id: "s8",
          label: "Techniques for Measuring Cost",
        },
        {
          id: "s9",
          label: "Disclosure Requirements",
        },
        {
          id: "s10",
          label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates",
        },
        {
          id: "s11",
          label: "IFRS vs US GAAP",
        },
        {
          id: "s12",
          label: "Statement Mapping",
        },
        {
          id: "s13",
          label: "Related Standards",
        },
        {
          id: "s14",
          label: "Glossary",
        },
        {
          id: "history",
          label: "History & Development",
        },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <!-- ============ SECTION 1 — CONCEPTUAL FRAMEWORK LINK ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Why inventory measurement is a matching exercise</div>
        <div class="principle">Inventory is a textbook Framework asset: a present economic resource controlled by the entity, from a past purchase or production event, with the potential to produce economic benefits through sale. IAS 2 exists to ensure the cost of that resource is matched against the revenue it generates in the SAME period.</div>
        <div class="elabel">HOW INVENTORY MAPS TO THE FRAMEWORK'S ELEMENTS</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">Control</strong> — legal ownership is common, but not required; control (e.g. via consignment terms) is the real test</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">Past event</strong> — purchase or production of the goods</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">Future economic benefit</strong> — cash inflows from eventual sale in the ordinary course of business</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">The lower-of-cost-and-NRV rule (Section 6) is a direct application of the Framework's prudence concept operating within neutrality — assets are not carried above the amount expected to be recovered through sale.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Measurement principles applied by IAS 2</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 2 uses historical cost as its foundation, but caps it at net realisable value — a hybrid approach the Framework explicitly permits when historical cost alone would overstate the asset.</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Framework basis</span><span>Used by IAS 2 for…</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Historical cost</span><span>Initial measurement — purchase cost, conversion cost, other costs to bring inventory to present location/condition</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Net realisable value (a current-value-adjacent basis)</span><span>Ceiling applied at each reporting date — write-down if NRV falls below cost</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Fair value less costs to sell (point of harvest)</span><span>Agricultural produce transferred in from IAS 41 — becomes IAS 2's "cost"</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout info" style="margin-bottom:0;">ℹ️ <strong style="font-weight:500">Qualitative characteristics in action:</strong> the lower-of-cost-and-NRV test balances <strong style="font-weight:500">relevance</strong> (reflecting a genuine decline in recoverable value) against <strong style="font-weight:500">faithful representation</strong> (never allowing inventory to be carried above what it can realistically generate).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 2 — SCOPE & DEFINITIONS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope &amp; Applicability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What IAS 2 covers</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 2 applies to all inventories, EXCEPT those specifically scoped out below. Inventories are assets held for sale in the ordinary course of business, in the process of production for such sale, or in the form of materials/supplies to be consumed in production or rendering of services.</div>
        <div class="elabel">CLASSIC CLASSES OF INVENTORY</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;margin-bottom:9px;">Merchandise purchased for resale; land and other property held for resale; finished goods produced; work in progress; raw materials and supplies awaiting use in the production process.</div>
        <div class="elabel" style="margin-top:9px;">SCOPE EXCLUSIONS — GOVERNED BY OTHER STANDARDS</div>
        <div class="cross-list">
          <span>Financial instruments → <strong style="font-weight:500">IFRS 9</strong></span>
          <span>Biological assets and agricultural produce at the point of harvest → <strong style="font-weight:500">IAS 41</strong> (fair value less costs to sell governs UP TO harvest; IAS 2 takes over AFTER)</span>
          <span>Work in progress arising under construction contracts, including directly related service contracts → <strong style="font-weight:500">IFRS 15</strong></span>
          <span>Measurement of inventories held by commodity broker-traders, measured at FV less costs to sell → excluded from IAS 2's measurement rules (only its disclosure rules apply)</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions you must know</div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Net realisable value (NRV)</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">The estimated selling price in the ordinary course of business, LESS the estimated costs of completion and the estimated costs necessary to make the sale.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Fair value</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">The price that would be received to sell an asset in an orderly transaction between market participants at the measurement date — a MARKET-based measure, distinct from NRV which is entity-specific.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Cost of inventories</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">All costs of purchase, costs of conversion, and other costs incurred in bringing inventories to their present location and condition.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;">
          <div class="glossary-term">Commodity broker-traders</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">Those who buy or sell commodities for others or on their own account, and whose inventories are principally acquired with the purpose of selling in the near future and generating a profit from fluctuations in price or broker-traders' margin.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 3 — RECOGNITION CRITERIA ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Recognition Criteria</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">When inventory is recognised — and derecognised</div>
        <div class="principle">Inventory is recognised as an asset when the entity has CONTROL over the goods (usually, but not always, coinciding with legal title/risk and reward transfer), and it is probable future economic benefits will flow to the entity, with cost reliably measurable.</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div class="spec-box" style="margin-bottom:0;">
            <div class="spec-title" style="color:var(--color-text-success);">Common recognition trigger points</div>
            <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.5;">Goods purchased "FOB shipping point" are recognised by the buyer from the shipping date; "FOB destination" goods are recognised only on arrival — control, not just physical possession, drives the answer.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="spec-box" style="margin-bottom:0;">
            <div class="spec-title" style="color:var(--color-text-success);">Derecognition</div>
            <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.5;">Inventory is derecognised (expensed as cost of sales) in the SAME period the related revenue is recognised — the direct matching principle central to IAS 2's design.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn" style="margin-top:8px;margin-bottom:0;">⚠️ <strong style="font-weight:600">Consignment inventory</strong> remains on the CONSIGNOR's books (not the holder's) until sold to a third party, because control has not transferred to the party holding the physical goods — a frequently tested scope/control judgement.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 4 — COST OF INVENTORIES (with worked example) ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Cost of Inventories — What's Included and Excluded</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">The three cost components</div>
        <div class="formula-box">
          <div class="f-eq">Cost = Costs of purchase + Costs of conversion + Other costs to bring to present location and condition</div>
        </div>
        <div class="elabel">COSTS OF PURCHASE — INCLUDE</div>
        <div class="check-list" style="margin-bottom:9px;">
          <span>Purchase price, import duties and other non-recoverable taxes</span>
          <span>Transport, handling, and other costs directly attributable to acquisition</span>
          <span style="text-decoration:line-through;color:var(--color-text-tertiary);">LESS: trade discounts, rebates, and similar items</span>
        </div>
        <div class="elabel">COSTS OF CONVERSION — INCLUDE</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Costs directly related to units of production (direct labour, direct materials)</span>
          <span>Systematic allocation of FIXED production overheads, based on NORMAL capacity</span>
          <span>Systematic allocation of VARIABLE production overheads, based on ACTUAL usage</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Costs EXCLUDED from inventory — always expensed</div>
        <div class="cross-list" style="margin-bottom:9px;">
          <span>Abnormal amounts of wasted materials, labour, or other production costs</span>
          <span>Storage costs, UNLESS necessary in the production process before a further production stage</span>
          <span>Administrative overheads that do not contribute to bringing inventories to present location/condition</span>
          <span>Selling costs</span>
          <span>Foreign exchange differences arising directly on the recent acquisition of inventories invoiced in a foreign currency (general rule — not capitalised, per IAS 21)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 1: Fixed overhead allocation using normal capacity</div>
          <div class="wk-body">
            A factory has normal production capacity of 10,000 units per month. Fixed production overheads are $50,000 per month. In a particular month, actual production is only 8,000 units due to a temporary equipment issue.
          </div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            <strong>Fixed overhead per unit</strong> = $50,000 ÷ 10,000 (NORMAL capacity, not actual) = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-info);">$5/unit</strong><br>
            Allocated to the 8,000 units produced = 8,000 × $5 = $40,000 → capitalised in inventory<br>
            Unallocated overhead = $50,000 − $40,000 = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-danger);">$10,000 → expensed immediately</strong> as a period cost (reflects idle capacity, not further capitalised)
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 5 — COST FORMULAS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Cost Formulas — FIFO, Weighted Average &amp; Specific Identification</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">The three permitted approaches</div>
        <div class="dot-list" style="margin-bottom:9px;">
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">Specific identification</strong> — MANDATORY for items that are not ordinarily interchangeable, and goods/services produced and segregated for specific projects</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">FIFO</strong> — assumes items purchased/produced first are sold first; closing inventory reflects most RECENT costs</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">Weighted average cost</strong> — cost of each item determined from the weighted average of similar items at the beginning of the period and those purchased/produced during the period</span>
        </div>
        <div class="callout danger" style="margin-bottom:0;">✕ <strong style="font-weight:600">LIFO (last-in, first-out) is explicitly PROHIBITED</strong> under IFRS — one of the clearest, most heavily examined IFRS vs US GAAP differences (see Section 11).</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Worked example — FIFO vs weighted average</div>
        <div class="worked">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 2: Comparing FIFO and weighted average</div>
          <div class="wk-body">
            Opening inventory: 100 units @ $10 = $1,000. Purchases: 200 units @ $12 = $2,400. Sales during the period: 250 units. Total units available: 300; closing inventory: 50 units.
          </div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            <strong>FIFO:</strong> closing inventory = 50 units from the LAST purchase batch @ $12 = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-info);">$600</strong><br>
            <strong>Weighted average:</strong> average cost = (1,000 + 2,400) ÷ 300 = $11.33/unit; closing inventory = 50 × $11.33 = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-info);">$567</strong> (rounded)<br>
            The SAME cost formula must be applied to all inventories of similar nature and use to the entity; a different formula may be justified for inventories with a different nature or use.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">The chosen formula is an accounting POLICY (IAS 8) and must be disclosed and applied consistently across periods, subject to the "similar nature and use" test permitting a different formula for genuinely different categories.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 6 — NET REALISABLE VALUE ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Net Realisable Value &amp; Write-Downs</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Lower of cost and NRV — applied item by item</div>
        <div class="formula-box">
          <div class="f-label">GENERAL RULE</div>
          <div class="f-eq">NRV = Estimated selling price − Estimated costs of completion − Estimated costs necessary to make the sale</div>
        </div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;margin-bottom:9px;">The comparison of cost and NRV is made on an ITEM-BY-ITEM basis (or by group of similar/related items) — NOT by broad classification of all inventories, and NOT based on total inventory value.</div>
        <div class="callout warn" style="margin-bottom:0;">⚠️ Materials and supplies held for use in production are NOT written down below cost if the finished products they'll be incorporated into are still expected to sell at or above cost — a specific carve-out that is a common exam trap.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Worked example — write-down and reversal</div>
        <div class="worked">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 3: NRV write-down, then partial reversal</div>
          <div class="wk-body">
            Product X: cost $80/unit, 500 units. At year-end 20X1, estimated selling price has fallen to $70/unit with $5/unit selling costs (NRV = $65). At year-end 20X2, selling prices recover; NRV rises back to $78/unit.
          </div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            <strong>20X1:</strong> Lower of cost ($80) and NRV ($65) = $65/unit. Write-down = (80 − 65) × 500 = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-danger);">$7,500 expense</strong>, inventory carried at $65 × 500 = $32,500.<br>
            <strong>20X2:</strong> New NRV ($78) is still below original cost ($80), so inventory is remeasured at the LOWER of original cost and new NRV = $78/unit. Reversal = (78 − 65) × 500 = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-success);">$6,500 reversal</strong> (reduces cost of sales), capped at the amount of the original write-down.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Reversals are permitted (unlike PPE impairments in some cases) but are strictly capped at the original write-down amount — inventory can never be written back up above original cost.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 7 — RECOGNITION AS AN EXPENSE ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Recognition as an Expense</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">The matching mechanics and journal entries</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;margin-bottom:9px;">When inventories are sold, their carrying amount is recognised as an expense (cost of sales) in the period the related revenue is recognised. Write-downs to NRV, and all inventory losses, are recognised as an expense in the period the write-down or loss occurs.</div>
        <div class="elabel">JOURNAL ENTRIES — SALE OF INVENTORY</div>
        <div class="entry"><span class="dr">Dr</span><div class="etxt">Cost of sales (cost of goods sold)<div class="badges"><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></div></div></div>
        <div class="entry"><span class="cr">Cr</span><div class="etxt">Inventory<div class="badges"><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></div></div></div>
        <div class="div"></div>
        <div class="elabel">JOURNAL ENTRY — WRITE-DOWN TO NRV</div>
        <div class="entry"><span class="dr">Dr</span><div class="etxt">Cost of sales / inventory write-down expense<div class="badges"><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></div></div></div>
        <div class="entry"><span class="cr">Cr</span><div class="etxt">Inventory (or allowance for NRV write-down)<div class="badges"><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></div></div></div>
        <div class="note">Some amounts, such as materials/components used in constructing a self-built asset, allocate inventory cost to the COST of another asset instead — e.g. capitalised into PPE under IAS 16 rather than expensed.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 8 — TECHNIQUES FOR MEASURING COST ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Techniques for Measuring Cost</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Standard cost and retail method — practical approximations</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">Standard cost method</strong> — takes into account normal levels of materials, labour, efficiency, and capacity utilisation, regularly reviewed and revised if conditions change; used ONLY if results approximate actual cost</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">Retail method</strong> — used in the retail industry for large numbers of rapidly changing items with similar margins where it's impracticable to use other costing methods; inventory value is reduced by an appropriate gross margin percentage from its sales value</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Both methods are convenience techniques for MEASURING cost, not separate cost FORMULAS — they must still produce a result reasonably approximating actual historical cost.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Worked example — retail method</div>
        <div class="worked">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 4: Retail method calculation</div>
          <div class="wk-body">
            A retailer's inventory has a retail selling price of $120,000. The standard gross margin across the relevant department is 35%.
          </div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            <strong>Estimated cost</strong> = Retail value × (1 − gross margin %) = 120,000 × (1 − 0.35) = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-info);">$78,000</strong>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">This is a practical expedient commonly seen in retail/supermarket exam scenarios, and is tested alongside the general lower-of-cost-and-NRV rule.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 9 — DISCLOSURE ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Required disclosures</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">GENERAL DISCLOSURES</div>
            <div class="check-list">
              <span>The accounting policies adopted, including the cost formula(s) used</span>
              <span>Total carrying amount of inventories, and the carrying amount in classifications appropriate to the entity (e.g. merchandise, raw materials, WIP, finished goods)</span>
              <span>Carrying amount of inventories carried at fair value less costs to sell</span>
              <span>Amount of inventories recognised as an expense during the period (cost of sales)</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">WRITE-DOWN &amp; RELATED DISCLOSURES</div>
            <div class="check-list">
              <span>Amount of any write-down of inventories recognised as an expense in the period</span>
              <span>Amount of any reversal of a write-down, and the circumstances/events that led to the reversal</span>
              <span>Carrying amount of inventories pledged as security for liabilities</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 10 — JUDGMENTS & ESTIMATES ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where management judgement matters most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Estimated selling price, costs of completion, and costs to sell used in the NRV calculation</span>
              <span>"Normal capacity" used to allocate fixed production overheads (a range is often used, averaged over several periods to smooth abnormal fluctuations)</span>
              <span>Obsolescence and slow-moving provisions for inventory unlikely to sell at expected prices</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Whether the entity has "control" over goods in transit or on consignment, driving the recognition timing</span>
              <span>Whether costs of conversion overhead allocations reflect genuinely "abnormal" wastage requiring immediate expensing</span>
              <span>Whether inventories of a genuinely different nature/use justify a different cost formula</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn" style="margin-top:8px;margin-bottom:0;">⚠️ Determining "normal capacity" is one of the most subjective areas of IAS 2 — using ACTUAL production instead in a low-output period would understate the abnormal-cost expense and overstate inventory, a common examiner trap.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 11 — IFRS vs US GAAP ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>IFRS vs US GAAP — Key Differences</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — IAS 2 vs US GAAP (ASC 330)</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IAS 2)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">US GAAP</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>LIFO</span><span>PROHIBITED</span><span>Permitted, and commonly used (tax advantages in rising-price environments)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Write-down ceiling</span><span>Lower of cost and NRV</span><span>Lower of cost and market (for LIFO/retail method users) or lower of cost and NRV (for others, since ASU 2015-11)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Reversal of write-downs</span><span>Permitted, capped at original write-down</span><span>PROHIBITED — once written down, the new value becomes the new cost basis, never reversed</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Item-level vs aggregate NRV testing</span><span>Item-by-item (or similar group)</span><span>Generally consistent with item-level, though practice varies by method used</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">The LIFO prohibition and the write-down reversal prohibition are the two most heavily tested IFRS/US GAAP differences for this standard — LIFO's tax-driven popularity in the US creates a structural, non-converging gap between the two frameworks.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 12 — STATEMENT MAPPING TABLE ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping — Where Every Inventory Event Lands</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Comprehensive event-to-statement mapping table</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event / transaction</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Purchase of inventory (on credit or cash)</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Recognised at cost; payable/cash adjusted</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Conversion costs incurred (labour, overhead allocation)</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Capitalised into WIP/finished goods per normal-capacity allocation</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Sale of inventory — cost of sales recognised</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span> <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Matched to the period revenue is recognised (IFRS 15)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Write-down to NRV</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span> <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Expensed immediately; inventory carrying amount reduced</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Reversal of a prior write-down</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span> <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Reduces cost of sales; capped at original write-down</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Abnormal wastage / idle capacity costs</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Expensed immediately, never capitalised into inventory</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Transfer from biological assets at point of harvest (IAS 41)</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Recognised at IAS 41 fair value at harvest, which becomes IAS 2 deemed cost</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Cash paid for inventory purchases</span><span><span class="badge bs">Cash Flow Statement</span></span><span>Operating activities outflow (IAS 7)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Change in inventory balance during the period</span><span><span class="badge bs">Cash Flow Statement</span></span><span>Working capital adjustment within operating activities (indirect method)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Accounting policy (cost formula), classification breakdown, write-down disclosures</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Mandatory regardless of formula or industry</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 13 — RELATED STANDARDS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards — Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">How IAS 2 connects across the IFRS body of literature</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 41</span><span>Agricultural produce is measured at fair value less costs to sell UP TO the point of harvest under IAS 41; that amount becomes the deemed "cost" IAS 2 uses thereafter</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IFRS 15</span><span>Determines WHEN revenue (and therefore matching cost of sales) is recognised; also governs work in progress under long-term contracts, which is scoped OUT of IAS 2</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 21</span><span>Governs foreign exchange differences on inventory purchases invoiced in foreign currency — generally NOT capitalised into inventory cost</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 23</span><span>Borrowing costs directly attributable to inventories that take a substantial period to get ready for sale (qualifying assets, e.g. maturing whisky/wine) are capitalised into inventory cost</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 16</span><span>Inventory consumed in constructing a self-built item of PPE is transferred out of inventory and capitalised as part of that asset's cost instead of being expensed</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IFRS 9</span><span>Financial instruments (e.g. commodity derivatives used to hedge inventory price risk) are excluded from IAS 2 and follow IFRS 9's own rules</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 1</span><span>Determines presentation of inventory within current assets on the SOFP, and cost of sales presentation choices (by nature vs by function)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 8</span><span>Governs treatment of a change in cost formula, which is treated as a change in accounting policy, applied retrospectively</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 14 — GLOSSARY ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="three-col">
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Costs of conversion</div><div class="glossary-def">Costs directly related to units of production, plus a systematic allocation of fixed and variable production overheads.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Normal capacity</div><div class="glossary-def">The production expected to be achieved on average over a number of periods/seasons under normal circumstances, taking into account planned maintenance downtime.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">By-products</div><div class="glossary-def">Products of relatively minor value arising alongside a main product from a joint production process — often measured at NRV, deducted from the main product's cost.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Joint products</div><div class="glossary-def">Products produced simultaneously from the same raw material/process, each with significant relative sales value — costs allocated between them on a rational, consistent basis (e.g. relative sales value).</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Retail method</div><div class="glossary-def">A cost measurement technique deducting an appropriate gross margin percentage from retail sales value to approximate cost, used in the retail industry.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Standard cost</div><div class="glossary-def">A predetermined cost based on normal levels of materials, labour, efficiency, and capacity utilisation, regularly reviewed and revised.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="legend">
      <div class="ltitle">Financial statement key</div>
      <div class="lrow">
        <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span> Statement of financial position (balance sheet)
        <span class="badge bp">SOPL</span> Statement of profit or loss (income statement)
        <span class="badge bo">OCI</span> Other comprehensive income
        <span class="badge be">Equity</span> Statement of changes in equity
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
